{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'rg -n \"''^'\"\\\\*\\\\*AC-|\"'^### |''^## \" docs/spec-build/v2/epics/07--archive-and-derived-views/epic.md'","aggregated_output":"9:## User Profile\n26:## Feature Overview\n41:## Current Implemented Baseline\n61:## Scope\n63:### In Scope\n78:### Out of Scope\n89:### Assumptions\n103:## Flows & Requirements\n105:### 1. Capturing Finalized Archive Entries\n120:**AC-1.1:** The platform stores finalized archive entries at low-level grain.\n135:**AC-1.2:** The canonical archive supports the PRD entry taxonomy.\n146:**AC-1.3:** Archive entries preserve order within one process.\n157:**AC-1.4:** Archive entries can link to related process, artifact, source, or tool context without requiring those related records to remain current.\n168:### 2. Keeping Streaming State Separate from Canonical Archive\n182:**AC-2.1:** Raw streaming deltas are not stored as canonical archive entries.\n189:**AC-2.2:** Interrupted partial objects are not archived as finalized entries.\n200:**AC-2.3:** Finalized entries may be created from completed live objects.\n211:### 3. Reading and Reopening the Canonical Archive\n225:**AC-3.1:** The user can read finalized archive entries for one process.\n236:**AC-3.2:** Reopening a process restores archive entries from durable state.\n247:**AC-3.3:** Archive reads enforce project and process access boundaries.\n258:**AC-3.4:** One degraded archive entry does not hide healthy archive entries.\n265:### 4. Deriving Turns from Archive Entries\n283:**AC-4.1:** The platform can derive turns from archived entries.\n294:**AC-4.2:** Each derived turn references the archive entries it groups.\n301:**AC-4.3:** Turn derivation does not mutate canonical archive entries.\n308:**AC-4.4:** Turn derivation handles incomplete or degraded groupings without failing the whole turn view.\n315:### 5. Producing Minimal Structural Views over Turns\n334:**AC-5.1:** The platform can expose a non-summarizing structural derived view\n343:**AC-5.2:** Derived views identify structural boundaries for the turns they\n352:**AC-5.3:** Derived views preserve provenance back to turns and archive entries.\n363:**AC-5.4:** Derived views do not replace or delete full-fidelity archive entries.\n370:**AC-5.5:** Derived-view generation can degrade without corrupting canonical archive state.\n381:### 6. Connecting Archive Entries to Source and Artifact Provenance\n396:**AC-6.1:** Archive entries can show related artifact-version provenance when available.\n403:**AC-6.2:** Archive entries can show related source provenance when available.\n410:**AC-6.3:** Missing related provenance does not hide archive truth.\n421:### 7. Returning Later and Handling Archive Degradation\n434:**AC-7.1:** Archive, turn, and derived-view reads are restorable after reload.\n445:**AC-7.2:** A derived-view failure does not hide canonical archive entries.\n452:**AC-7.3:** Archive reads remain bounded for long processes.\n461:## Data Contracts\n463:### Browser Routes\n474:### Endpoints\n483:### Archive Entry\n505:### Archive Response\n516:### Derived Turn\n529:### Turn Response\n538:### Derived Archive View\n555:### Derived Views Response\n561:### Error Responses\n574:## Dependencies\n596:## Non-Functional Requirements\n598:### Performance\n607:### Security\n615:### Reliability\n625:### Observability\n636:## Tech Design Questions\n662:## Recommended Story Breakdown\n668:### Story 0: Foundation (Infrastructure)\n675:### Story 1: Canonical Archive Entry Persistence\n687:### Story 2: Finalization Boundary Between Live State and Archive\n698:### Story 3: Archive Read and Reopen Surface\n710:### Story 4: Turn Derivation\n722:### Story 5: Minimal Structural Views over Turns\n735:### Story 6: Archive Provenance Coherence\n746:### Story 7: Reopen and Degraded Archive State\n759:## Validation Checklist\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
