/projects/platform-store.ts:3261:    turns: DerivedTurn[];\\n./apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts:3266:      args.turns\\n./apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts:3277:    const turns = [...(this.archiveTurnsByProcessId.get(args.processId) ?? [])].sort(\\n./apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts:3281:      items: turns,\\n./apps/platform/server/services/projects/platform-store.ts:3288:      turns: paginated.items,\\n./tests/service/server/provider-adapter-registry.test.ts:64:  it('returns the supplied adapter for any providerKind', () => {\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/00-foundation.md:106:| Get authenticated user | GET | `/auth/me` | Returns the current authenticated user or 401 if not authenticated |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/00-foundation.md:107:| Sign out authenticated user | POST | `/auth/logout` | Ends the current authenticated session and returns the browser to a signed-out state |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/00-foundation.md:108:| List accessible projects | GET | `/api/projects` | Returns the projects the current user can access |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/00-foundation.md:110:| Get project shell data | GET | `/api/projects/{projectId}` | Returns project identity plus project shell summaries |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/00-foundation.md:150:| `X-CSRF-Token` header | string | yes | Browser returns the shell bootstrap CSRF token on `POST /auth/logout` |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/decision-memo.md:46:| Project shell partial failures | Use one aggregated project-shell bootstrap endpoint that returns per-section envelopes: `processes`, `artifacts`, and `sourceAttachments` each carry `status`, `items`, and optional `error`; return HTTP `200` when project identity/access succeeds, and reserve request failure codes for auth/access/not-found failures | Lock now | This is the cleanest way to satisfy the epic's partial-failure requirements without forcing client fan-out. It should be documented as a design-time clarification/deviation from the epic's simpler array-only contract. |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:5:The client work is intentionally lighter than the process surface work. Review has no websocket subscription, no same-session action reconciliation, no live envelope. A durable bootstrap returns the workspace; reload replays the bootstrap; one HTTP request per navigation. What the client gains instead is surface richness — markdown rendering container, Mermaid hydration, version switching, package member navigation, and the single-action export trigger — but all of that sits on straightforward fetch-and-render plumbing.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:83:- `patchReviewTarget` — used when a version or member switch response returns a new target (keeps bootstrap context intact, replaces only the target and selection)\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:120:The `getReviewWorkspace` call drives the initial bootstrap. `getArtifactReview` and `getPackageReview` are the targeted in-page switches (version switching within an artifact, member switching within a package) — they return just the target-level payload without re-fetching the entire workspace envelope. `exportPackage` is a POST that returns the export response metadata (phase 1 of the two-phase export flow); the user's browser then GETs `response.downloadUrl` to stream the `.mpkz` bytes (phase 2). The `getReviewWorkspace` call is not invoked from `exportPackage` — phase 2 is a direct anchor navigation to the signed download URL.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:276:| TC-6.2c | revoked access blocks direct URL | `tests/service/client/review-workspace-page.test.ts` | bootstrap returns 403 | route-level unavailable state renders |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:507:- **Two-phase flow**: phase 1 returns JSON metadata; phase 2 is a direct anchor navigation to the signed URL. The client's `review-workspace-api.ts` handles only phase 1. Phase 2 is the user clicking the rendered `<a href>` — no client-side `fetch` is involved\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:509:- **Expired URL handling**: if the user waits past `expiresAt` and clicks the download link, the server returns 404 at phase 2. The client has no direct signal from that 404 (the browser handles the failed navigation). The user re-clicks the export button to re-trigger phase 1 and get a fresh URL\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:521:| TC-5.3a | export failure does not close review | `tests/service/client/export-trigger.test.ts` | API returns 503 | failure affordance renders; package review panel remains operational |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-client.md:522:| TC-5.3b | expired export requires re-export | `tests/service/client/export-trigger.test.ts` | user clicks export twice; first response expires, second returns fresh | both clicks POST; second click renders fresh download link |\\n./tests/service/server/processes-api.test.ts:308:    turns: DerivedTurn[];\\n./tests/service/server/processes-api.test.ts:313:      turns: [],\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:100:**AC-1.4:** Authenticated project surfaces provide a sign-out action that ends the current session, returns the user to a signed-out state, and blocks access to project data until the user signs in again.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:105:  - Then: The platform ends the current session and returns the user to a signed-out state\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:122:| Get authenticated user | GET | `/auth/me` | Returns the current authenticated user or 401 if not authenticated |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:123:| Sign out authenticated user | POST | `/auth/logout` | Ends the current authenticated session and returns the browser to a signed-out state |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:124:| List accessible projects | GET | `/api/projects` | Returns the projects the current user can access |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:125:| Get project shell data | GET | `/api/projects/{projectId}` | Returns project identity plus project shell summaries |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/01-authenticated-project-entry.md:150:`POST /auth/logout` returns `200` with a logout redirect payload after the authenticated session has been invalidated successfully.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/02-project-creation-and-open.md:61:  - Then: No project is created and the user returns to the project index unchanged\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/02-project-creation-and-open.md:97:**AC-6.1:** Canceling project creation or process creation creates no partial record and returns the user to a stable prior surface.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/02-project-creation-and-open.md:102:  - Then: No project record is created and the user returns to the project index\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/02-project-creation-and-open.md:116:| Get project shell data | GET | `/api/projects/{projectId}` | Returns project identity plus project shell summaries |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/stories/02-project-creation-and-open.md:158:**Success response:** `POST /api/projects` returns `201` with a `Project Shell Response` body for the newly created project. On initial create, each section envelope returns an empty `items` array with `status: empty`.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:43:| Optional `processId` on the shell route implied `PROCESS_NOT_FOUND` in the error table, but AC-6.2b requires the shell to remain usable when the selected process is missing | Query parameter contract, AC-6.2b | Aggregated shell bootstrap returns `200` for accessible projects and the client clears invalid process selection route state after comparing against `processes.items`; `PROCESS_NOT_FOUND` remains reserved for dedicated process resources | Resolved — clarified |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:98:The route returns a single `ProjectShellResponse`, but each section reader is a\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:172:The projection returns:\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:255:| `/projects` | Fastify shell route | Returns authenticated shell HTML or redirects to sign-in |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:256:| `/projects/:projectId` | Fastify shell route | Returns authenticated shell HTML or project unavailable/auth redirect |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/01--project-and-process-shell/tech-design.md:290:1. The shell bootstrap still returns `200` for an accessible project, and the\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:48:  userId: 'workos-user-turns-1',\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:49:  workosUserId: 'workos-user-turns-1',\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:166:describe('turn derivation service and route', () => {\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:167:  it('TC-4.1a turns derived from archive', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:180:      turns: [\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:215:      turns: [],\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:239:      turns: [\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:303:      turns: [\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:352:    const turns = await service.rebuildTurns({ projectId, processId });\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:354:    expect(turns).toEqual([\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:378:  it('turn-cache rebuild preserves stable turn provenance for derived views', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/turn-derivation.test.ts:425:      throw new Error('Expected the initial rebuild to produce two turns.');\\n./tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts:506:  ])('TC-5.3b returns REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND when the signed export URL token is $label', async ({\\n./tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts:900:  it('TC-4.1a returns informing source provenance', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/source-management-api.test.ts:952:  it('TC-4.2a returns receiving source provenance', async () => {\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/team-impl-log.md:103:| 19 | `story-continue` envelope returns `continuationHandle: null` on follow-up pass | **OPEN (minor)** | At 2026-04-23 ~11:20Z, Story 1 `story-continue` finished `ready-for-verification` cleanly, but the result envelope's `continuationHandle` field is null. The codex session underneath the continue call is obviously still valid (the continue succeeded against the same `019db9dc-dacb-76f3-b2d3-56a6594da7bb` handle), the CLI just doesn't echo it back. Orchestrator workaround: continue using the last known handle from the log's `Current Continuation Handles` section — the session id from the first `story-implement` envelope is still the authoritative one. Fix direction: surface the reused handle in every follow-up envelope so recovery doesn't need to walk back to the initial implementor result. |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/team-impl-log.md:178:  - Follow-up 3 (error-path guard + explicit member unavailability): `artifacts/06-.../009-continue.json` — added requestId guard to error catch paths + explicit memberId returns unavailable instead of fallback\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/team-impl-log.md:189:  - (rd 2) explicit missing memberId silently falls back → **fixed** (continue 009: returns unavailable for explicit-but-missing member)\\n./apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/local-provider-adapter.ts:30:   * Runs `git clone` (or equivalent) for a source attachment. Returns `null`\\n./apps/platform/server/services/processes/environment/local-provider-adapter.ts:41:   * Executes the bootstrap TS script in the working tree. Returns the child\\n./apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:28:  turns: processArchiveTurnsApiPathnamePattern,\\n./apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:127:    '/api/projects/:projectId/processes/:processId/archive/turns',\\n./apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:145:        const turns = await app.turnDerivationService.getTurns({\\n./apps/platform/server/routes/archive.ts:153:        return reply.code(200).send(turns);\\n./tests/service/server/projects-api.test.ts:75:  it('TC-1.2a returns only accessible projects sorted by lastUpdatedAt descending', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/projects-api.test.ts:154:  it('returns an empty project list for an actor with no memberships', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/projects-api.test.ts:221:  it('returns an empty shell bootstrap for an accessible project', async () => {\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:179:- fake Convex File Storage that returns predetermined markdown byte streams in response to signed URLs\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:182:- Mermaid library mock that records calls, returns controllable SVG fixtures, and simulates render failures\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:232:| TC-1.1a | `TC-1.1a bootstrap returns review workspace for process with reviewable artifact` | Process with one durable artifact version | GET bootstrap | response includes artifact in availableTargets |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:234:| TC-1.1d | `TC-1.1d multiple targets open in selection state` | Process with two targets, no selection | GET bootstrap | response returns availableTargets; `target` omitted |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:235:| TC-1.1e | `TC-1.1e zero-target direct route opens empty` | Process with zero targets | GET bootstrap | response returns empty availableTargets; `target` omitted |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:248:| `bootstrap returns 401 when unauthenticated` | Route-level auth invariant |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:249:| `bootstrap returns 404 PROCESS_NOT_FOUND for invalid processId` | Standard route failure path |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:268:| `returns unavailable when query versionId does not exist` | Robust input handling |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:269:| `returns unsupported content kind without attempting content fetch` | Efficiency + correctness for unsupported fallback |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:332:| `render error returns structured bodyStatus: error without throwing` | Bounded failure invariant |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:363:| TC-6.2c | `TC-6.2c revoked access on direct URL` | Bootstrap returns 403 | Mount page | route-level unavailable state |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:452:| `cache hit returns same SVG without re-calling mermaid.render` | LRU cache behavior |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:465:| TC-5.3a | `TC-5.3a export failure does not close review` | API returns 503 | Click export | failure affordance renders; surrounding panel remains operational |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:585:| `reopening with stale selection (deleted version) returns unavailable without leaking prior content` | Graceful degradation |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:600:| Export preparation (phase 1) begins within 2 s | `phase-1 POST returns within 2 s for 20-member package` | Call export route via `app.inject`; assert phase-1 response time under 2 s with Convex reads mocked at realistic fixture delay |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:700:**Scope:** `createPackageFromEntries` streaming API on the workspace package; tar hardening (gzip-bomb cap, per-entry + cumulative byte caps, entry-name filter, path-traversal rejection); two-phase export service (phase-1 POST returns signed URL JSON; phase-2 GET streams bytes); signed URL mint + verify; expired-URL 404; `mdvpkg` CLI polish; export trigger UI with download-link render; `_nav.md` manifest generation and round-trip.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/test-plan.md:745:7. Select a prior version and confirm the body re-renders; confirm the URL updates via `history.pushState` so browser back returns to the prior selection.\\n./tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts:552:  it('returns an unavailable selected member when an explicit memberId is missing', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/package-review-api.test.ts:668:  it('smoke returns a 20-member package review within the Story 4 timing target', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/archive-api.test.ts:209:  it('TC-3.3b missing process archive read returns not found', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/archive-api.test.ts:263:  it('invalid archive query returns INVALID_ARCHIVE_REQUEST', async () => {\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:148:| `export.service.ts` | NEW | Two-phase export: POST returns `ExportPackageResponse` JSON with a signed download URL; GET at that URL (handled by the same `routes/review.ts` module) streams the `.mpkz` archive through `createPackageFromEntries` into the Fastify reply body | `PlatformStore`, `@liminal-build/markdown-package`, `export-url-signing` | AC-5 |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:299:- **`apps/platform/server/services/processes/readers/materials-section.reader.ts`** — MODIFIED. Today this reader produces `ArtifactSummary` rows for the process work surface's materials envelope. Epic 4 extends its per-artifact projection to `getLatestArtifactVersion(artifactId)` and derive `currentVersionLabel` + `updatedAt` from that row. When the materials projection returns N artifacts, this costs N version-lookups; at first-cut material-list scale (≤ 20 items per process in Epic 2's NFR) this is acceptable. If a future epic materializes much larger materials lists, denormalization can be reintroduced as a performance optimization.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:618:Export is a **two-phase flow**. The POST `/export` endpoint returns a JSON metadata record with a signed GET download URL; the user's browser then GETs that URL to stream the `.mpkz` bytes. Expired URL retrieval returns 404 `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:689:  }): Promise<string>;  // returns the new artifactVersionId\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:700:  }): Promise<string>;  // returns the new packageSnapshotId\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:759:The renderer never throws — it returns `bodyStatus: 'error'` with a populated `bodyError` when markdown parsing or sanitization fails. Mermaid sanitization is a pre-render step (fence interception inside the markdown-it pipeline) and cannot error the whole artifact; a malformed Mermaid source yields a non-empty `MermaidBlock` entry that the client will fail to render on its own.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:788:`requestExport` validates that every package member is available (otherwise `REVIEW_EXPORT_NOT_AVAILABLE`), mints a signed token via `export-url-signing.ts`, and returns the `ExportPackageResponse`. No bytes are streamed in this phase.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:790:`downloadExport` verifies the token (rejects expired or invalid tokens with 404 `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`), re-validates the actor's access to the project/process, and returns a `Readable` that Fastify pipes into the reply body. Member content is streamed end-to-end via `createPackageFromEntries`.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:805:  verify(token: string): ExportTokenPayload | null;  // returns null on invalid/expired\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:815:The review workspace bootstrap is the entry point from either the process work surface's `review` action or a direct URL reopen. The server resolves project and process access, computes reviewability, resolves the target from query state, and returns the workspace envelope in one durable read.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:868:| TC-1.1d | multiple targets open in selection state | `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts` | process with two targets, no query state | response returns availableTargets only; `target` omitted |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:869:| TC-1.1e | zero-target direct route opens empty | `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts` | process with zero targets, direct review URL | response returns empty availableTargets |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:875:| TC-6.2a | missing artifact shows unavailable | `tests/service/server/review-workspace-api.test.ts` | artifact deleted after URL bookmarked | response returns target with status: unavailable |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:883:Artifact review resolves the full version history for one artifact, selects either the query-state version or the current version as default, fetches the selected version's content from Convex File Storage, and renders it through the markdown pipeline. When an artifact exists in the current process review context but has no reviewable version yet, the artifact-specific endpoint still returns the artifact identity with an empty version list; the review-workspace wrapper later maps that condition to `target.status: empty`.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:918:- if query state specifies a `versionId` that does not exist, the target-specific endpoint returns `404 REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND`; the **bootstrap** route is different and may still return a bounded `target.status: unavailable` workspace envelope when project/process context resolves\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:919:- `getArtifactVersionContentUrl` returns a Convex-issued stable URL (via `ctx.storage.getUrl()` wrapped in an `internalQuery`); Fastify fetches content over HTTP in the same request and does not cache the URL across requests. Access control lives at the route boundary\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:920:- content fetches time out at **`ARTIFACT_CONTENT_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS = 10_000`** (exported as a constant from `artifact-review.service.ts` so tests can patch it); if the fetch times out or otherwise fails, the response returns `bodyStatus: 'error'` with `bodyError: { code: 'REVIEW_RENDER_FAILED', message: ... }` while keeping the version list visible\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:941:The render pipeline turns a markdown string into sanitized HTML with Mermaid placeholders, emitting the raw Mermaid sources in a sidecar list for client-side hydration. The pipeline is pure: it takes bytes in, returns a structured result, and does not touch any store or external system.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1010:4. Returns `{ blockId, sanitizedSource }` for the sidecar\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1049:Additional non-TC tests for this flow: mermaid directive stripping rejects `%%{init}%%` line, rejects `%%{config}%%` line, rejects `%%{wrap}%%` line; DOMPurify config rejects inline `<style>` tags; markdown-it `html: false` rejects raw `<script>` tags; render error path returns structured error without throwing.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1055:Package review resolves one published snapshot, returns the ordered member list, and fetches the selected member's artifact version detail through the same markdown render pipeline as Flow 2. A package member's `artifactVersionId` is the immutable pin — selecting a member always loads that specific version, never the artifact's current version. The snapshot is opened from one process review context, but its pinned members may come from multiple processes in the same project.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1110:| **A target is selected but has no reviewable inner content** | `target` is present with `target.status: 'empty'` | Artifact identity resolved but the artifact has zero `artifactVersions` rows; the artifact endpoint returns an empty version list and the workspace wrapper maps that to `target.status: 'empty'`, per AC-2.4 |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1134:Export is a two-phase flow. Phase 1 is a POST that validates the export request, mints a signed download URL, and returns metadata. Phase 2 is a GET at the signed URL that streams the `.mpkz` archive bytes through `@liminal-build/markdown-package`'s `createPackageFromEntries` into the Fastify reply body. Splitting the two phases matches the spec's `ExportPackageResponse` contract (with `downloadUrl` + `expiresAt`), lets the client render a real download link before bytes flow, and gives expired-URL handling (AC-5.3b) a natural implementation.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1208:- **Download URL reuse semantics.** The signed URL is **reusable within the expiry window** — the same URL can be GET'd multiple times until `expiresAt`, each GET re-validates the token, re-checks access + exportability, and streams a fresh archive. No single-use enforcement. This lets users copy the download link, re-download after an interrupted transfer, or share within the expiry if they choose. After `expiresAt`, every GET returns 404 `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND` regardless of how many prior downloads succeeded.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1211:- **Mid-stream auth.** If the actor's access to the project/process was revoked between phase 1 and phase 2, phase 2 returns 403 `PROJECT_FORBIDDEN` at the start; no bytes stream.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1232:| TC-5.1a | export accepted and returns signed URL | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | exportable package | POST 200; response body is valid `ExportPackageResponse` with `downloadUrl`, `expiresAt`, `contentType: application/gzip`, `packageFormat: mpkz` |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1233:| TC-5.1a (phase 2) | export download streams valid tar.gz | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | POST result's downloadUrl | GET returns 200 `application/gzip`; stream extracts to valid archive with expected members |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1235:| TC-5.1b (server-side) | preflight rejects when any member unavailable | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | package with unavailable member | POST returns 409 `REVIEW_EXPORT_NOT_AVAILABLE` |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1239:| TC-5.3b | expired export requires re-export | `tests/service/server/review-export-api.test.ts` | signed URL with past `expiresAt` | GET returns 404 `REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND` |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1436:Members are listed in package order (by `position`). `parseManifest` returns a typed representation with `metadata.title`, `metadata.packageType`, `metadata.packageId`, `metadata.publishedAt` populated and all other `ManifestMetadata` fields absent; `scaffoldManifest` produces the markdown form from `{ snapshot, orderedMembers }` input.\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1559:- Render error path returns structured error result rather than throwing\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1560:- Artifact review returns artifact identity plus `versions: []` for zero-version artifacts without attempting a content fetch; the workspace wrapper is what maps that condition to `target.status: empty`\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1568:- `hasReviewableTargets` returns `false` for processes with zero artifact versions and zero package snapshots\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/04--artifact-review-and-package-surface/tech-design-server.md:1569:- `hasReviewableTargets` returns `true` for processes with at least one reviewable target of either kind\\n./tests/integration/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer-integration.test.ts:236:    it('updates an existing file and returns a new commit SHA distinct from the first commit', async () => {\\n./tests/integration/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer-integration.test.ts:276:    it('returns a failed outcome with a meaningful failureReason when the target ref does not exist', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:101:  it('commits successfully to a writable target ref and returns the resulting commit SHA', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:137:  it('creates the file on first commit when getContent returns 404', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:151:  it('returns failed with a refresh-suggesting failureReason when GitHub responds 409 conflict', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:165:  it('returns failed naming the auth issue when GitHub returns 401', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:178:  it('returns failed naming the auth issue when GitHub returns 403', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:191:  it('returns failed describing the underlying network error when getContent throws a non-HTTP error', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:214:  it('returns failed when the repositoryUrl is not a github.com URL', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/octokit-code-checkpoint-writer.test.ts:227:  it('returns failed when targetRef is null because direct write requires an explicit branch', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:135:  it('TC-2.1a returns the newest durable revision as the current version', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:586:  it('returns ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND when an explicit artifact version is unavailable', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:618:  it('TC-3.2b returns REVIEW_TARGET_NOT_FOUND when the process neither references nor pins the artifact', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:696:  it('TC-2.3b returns artifact versions newest to oldest', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:727:  it('TC-2.4a returns the no-version empty state for an owned artifact with no durable revisions', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:764:  it('TC-3.4c returns ARTIFACT_VERSION_NOT_FOUND when an explicit version is requested for a zero-version artifact', async () => {\\n./tests/service/server/artifact-review-api.test.ts:796:  it('returns a ready empty markdown review when a durable version body is zero-byte', async () => {\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-review-provenance-alignment/test-plan.md:223:| TC-3.4b | direct zero-version artifact review returns bounded empty state | Empty, not unavailable |\\n./docs/spec-build/v2/epics/05--artifact-model-and-revie